Strategies for the Sustainability of Stat Labs: A Case Study of Laboratory of Interdisciplinary Statistical Analysis, Lahore College for Women University Lahore, Pakistan (LISA-LCWU)
Asifa Kamal, Asma Zeb, Abeera Shakeel, and Naila Amjad
1 Introduction/Background
Cornfield referred to statistics as “the bedfellow of sciences,” and John Tukey stated that “the great thing about statistics is that you get to play in everyone’s backyard” (Love et al., 2017). It is undeniable that statistics is a multidisciplinary field, and collaboration between statistics and various disciplines is crucial. Recognizing the strength of statistics as a collaborative science, Dr. Eric Vance, the global director of the “The Laboratory for Interdisciplinary Statistical Analysis” (LISA, 2020) network, presented his idea of establishing 20 statistics and data science collaboration laboratories in developing countries called “stats lab.” These labs would serve as engines for development within those countries and globally by sharing good practices among the network. These stat labs organize short courses and workshops to improve statistical skills and data literacy, ultimately serving as infrastructure to support local researchers, businesses, government, and NGOs. The LISA 2020 program encourages new stat labs to create a strong foundation for their operations by building on their planning guide and by adapting best practices learned from other stat labs in the network. A modern statistical collaboration laboratory should have an interdisciplinary focus, state-of-the-art infrastructure, expertise in statistical analysis, promote research collaboration, provide education in data science, and be financially sustainable. These attributes will encourage collaboration among researchers from different fields, facilitate data-intensive research, provide consulting services, promote collaboration among researchers, develop a skilled workforce, and ensure long-term viability.
