Mission of these stat labs is three-fold. First and foremost is to prepare future generation of statisticians and data scientists as “effective interdisciplinary collaborators” who can help policy-makers in the solution of real-world problems. Collaborator, a statistician or data scientist is “someone possessing deep technical skills in the theory and methods of statistics or data science and effective collaboration skills who can move between theory and practice to work with domain experts to create solutions to research, business and policy challenges and achieve research, business and policy goals” or in other words “who can transform evidence into action” (Vance & Pruitt, 2022). Secondly, it was clarified by arguing that collaboration is not merely statistical consultancy or advice to researcher/development actor, it is the contribution of statistician/data scientist in the final outcome beyond consultancy (Love et al., 2017). These labs facilitate researchers and decision makers to make data-driven policies that have positive impact on society. Third is the capacity building for statistical skills and widen data literacy through workshops, trainings etc. Currently this is strong global network comprised of 35 labs in 10 countries in three continents (Africa, Brazil and Asia). LISA CU Boulder is center of this network and this stats lab network is sharing good practices as collaborators with each other and promoting interdisciplinary research culture through these stat-labs. One of the most esteemed institutions in Pakistan, Lahore College for Women University has an impressive enrollment of approximately 15,000 full-time students and a teaching faculty exceeding 500 members. Lahore College for Women University, established in May 1922 as an intermediate college. The university offers admission to students at the Intermediate, Graduate, Masters, and Ph.D. levels. In 1962, Lahore College for Women added Statistics as a subject and started the first class with only three students. Two years later, in 1964, the college introduced undergraduate-level classes with just eleven students. Today, over a thousand students at intermediate, undergraduate, graduate and post-graduate levels are benefiting from the Statistics Department. The department has experienced a significant increase in the number of students opting to study statistics at both intermediate, graduate and post-graduate levels due to its diverse applications in various fields. Dr. Asifa Kamal, from the Department of Statistics at Lahore College for Women University (LCWU), was selected to attend the LISA Symposium 2019 held in Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia, under the umbrella of LISA. The symposium aimed to provide training and create networks for Collaborative Statistical Laboratories, and LCWU had the opportunity to become a part of thirty partners, with Dr. Asifa Kamal becoming a member of the LISA network. The mission of LISA-LCWU Stat Lab is to produce trained statisticians capable of serving as interdisciplinary collaborators for quantitative research. Sustainable development in any area of life needs support from Statistics and Data Science that contribute potentially to expedite data-driven development (Vance & Pruitt, 2022). Immediate example in local scenario is control of COVID-19 expo


