2.1 Identification of Sustainability Problem of LISA-LCWU
Following two major challenges have been identified regarding sustainability of LISA-LCWU.
Challenge 1: Gap Between Domain Experts and Collaborators
LISA-LCWU has observed good participation from the university during its trainings, workshops, and other activities. However, there is a need to extend this participation beyond the boundaries of LCWU. Research students pursuing their Master’s and Ph.D. degrees and faculty members from other disciplines who are involved in research have not contacted LISA-LCWU as much as expected. Additionally, collaboration has been difficult as LISA-LCWU has not received any funded projects thus far. To overcome these challenges, there is a need to attract domain experts and build trust by showcasing the strengths and achievements.
Challenge 2: Demotivation of Collaborators
• Workload not Counted
It is very difficult to convince university to generate permanent post or reduce the workload of person who is managing lab due to budgetary problems. University authorities also not agreed to account collaboration workload in the regular workload. Even some domain experts are not willing to give credit to collaborators as co-authorship. Since collaborators are faculty members of Statistics department so they are not willing to take up extra workload. Also, students of Statistics department have not enough skills to collaborate in isolation with domain expert without mentorship of faculty.
• Monetary Benefits are not Associated with LISA Collaborators and Trainers
Collaborators from Statistics department found it difficult to manage collaboration activities along with their regular university duties. There was not any incentive except certificate of appreciation to motivate them to participate in collaborations and trainings on regular basis and it is not enough for motivation.
