3.4 Funds Generation for Self-Reliance
Funds are needed to provide nominal payments as tokens of recognition, along with certificates, to members of the LISA-LCWU team, resource persons for workshops, and those offering data analysis consultancy. Additionally, funds are essential for organizing events, as they play a vital role, much like blood in the human body. The government and educational stakeholders ought to allocate funds to create institutes or programs for statistics education in Nigeria (Adelodun & Awe, 2013). Following sources were proposed to get monetary self-reliance.
Token Fee for Workshops Participants
LISA-LCWU submitted an application to charge a fee for their one-week workshop, which was proposed to be 2 USD for statistics students and 5 USD for others. However, the competent authority has not yet approved the fee charging proposal. This fee charging strategy is considered to be a significant measure that can contribute to the sustainability of LISA-LCWU.
Applying for Grants
Funded projects not only provide strong foundation but also can be a key for sustainability of stat labs. Projects are helpful in the capacity building by solving real life data challenges and it is the best strategy for the training the junior statisticians and students (Love et al., 2017). If projects are funded, share in funds as a compensation of time and resources to senior faculty acting as mentor, students as collaborators and future collaborators as trainee can be used as motivation (Love et al., 2017).
Nominal Fee from Domain Experts for Collaboration on Projects
Co-authorship is great tool as substitute of monetary benefits for data analysis consultancy and it has been adopted. Charging a nominal fee to domain expert, for data analysis consultancy has not been approved by competent authority. It may demotivate the collaborators who spend a lot of time for solution without monetary benefits. Due to non-approval of charging fee for collaboration from higher authorities of university, webpage about collaboration has not been generated.
Data Analysis Consultancy Desk (An Alternative Strategy to Train Collaborators)
In the absence of monetary benefits, an alternative strategy was employed to facilitate collaboration projects and train collaborators. This involved organizing a two-day activity called the “Data Analysis Consultancy Desk” within the Statistics department. Under the supervision of the faculty of the Statistics department at LCWU, graduate and undergraduate students in the Statistics program collaborated with domain experts who registered for the activity. Even though the activity was only 2 days long, all mentors (faculty members of the Statistics department) and collaborators (students pursuing Bachelor’s and Master’s degrees in Statistics) provided free data analysis advice to 38 domain experts in a professional manner.


